#align(center, box(width: 90%, align(left)[
  *Summary.* The claim this plan serves is that a language like Ochr makes a good general-purpose language. That needs two guarantees, _consistency_, so that its proofs can be trusted, and _type safety_, so that accepted programs never crash or go wrong when run. The plan proves both by _meaning preservation_. Types are given meanings in a set model, in which `False` denotes the empty set. Every state the checker reaches while checking a definition is given a meaning too, as a function of what its unknowns stand for: an _assignment_ gives each unknown any element of its type's meaning, non-computable functions included. The meaning of running a term is its _reference run_, which applies the meanings of functions where the checker runs or closes off their bodies, and which is _strict_: it fails wherever a value would be put at a type it is not a member of. The argument then has two halves, which meet only at "the strict run succeeds". (I) A _fundamental lemma_ about the model alone, the counterpart of the soundness theorem of Lean's own metatheory: from a state of the model whose positions hold members of their types, a strict run that succeeds returns a member of its type and leaves such a state, and a proof that runs proves a true proposition. (II) A _simulation_: the checker's successful run of a term makes the strict run of the same term succeed, with the same meanings, the same live borrows and the same places lent to each. The checker's checks serve this half: each is what makes one strict point succeed, through the soundness of its comparison of types. An accepted definition therefore means an element of its type, and a closed proof of `False` would be an element of the empty set. Type safety reuses the same meanings: a closed run of an accepted function means what the reference run of its call says, and that run never goes wrong; what remains for it is chiefly a realizability relation for calls through function values (@sec-safety).

  *Where it stands (21 November 2026).* The paper tests of the route passed (@sec-tests). A Lean development against the real checker is under way (@sec-spine), and the theorem is not proved end to end. Half (I) is proved: the fundamental lemma holds for every clause of the strict run with nothing unproved beneath it, its context has an instance checked by Lean's kernel, and it has been applied to concrete runs. It was found wrong twice on the way (first true only vacuously, then false at a write to a field) and repaired both times. Half (II) is open, and its first step cannot be proved as things stand: the lemma tests a write at more futures than the relation between the two states compares, and nothing constrains the reference state in between. How to close that seam is the open design question (Q4, @sec-oq); the form the simulation's statements will take is otherwise settled. The soundness of the checker's comparison and of its computation of `Eq` is proved, on a branch, for values that hold no code. That the checker's tables only grow is proved for most of the machine. That a model of the data types exists for every accepted program is decomposed into nine pieces and not started. In the night of 20 to 21 November the mechanisation and an adversarial agent found three accepted proofs of `False` in the checker, in positivity and in dependent fields; all three are closed, and none was in an area the test suite exercised. Five were known before (@sec-risks). The hardest open parts are the seam, the simulation's steps for calls and stuck blocks, the frame property they rest on, and the existence of the data model. What remains is listed in @sec-order.
]))
